1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,631
Two traders. Same system, same trades.

2
00:00:09,081 --> 00:00:15,507
The first risks 10% of the account per trade. The second only 1%.

3
00:00:15,956 --> 00:00:21,286
Then come ten losses in a row. It happens, in every system.

4
00:00:21,735 --> 00:00:26,726
The first account is down 65%. The second only about 10%.

5
00:00:27,176 --> 00:00:30,650
The only difference was one number: how much was risked.

6
00:00:31,650 --> 00:00:36,561
Hello, this is episode four of the SPIDER Quant Trading series.

7
00:00:37,011 --> 00:00:42,888
Last time: drawdown, and how position size controls it.

8
00:00:43,338 --> 00:00:49,373
Today: size itself. How much to risk per trade, how to calculate it, and how much is too much.

9
00:00:50,373 --> 00:00:57,373
The biggest mistake: people pick the lot size first, whatever feels right.

10
00:00:57,823 --> 00:01:05,137
A quant thinks the other way. First decide: if this trade is wrong, how much am I willing to lose?

11
00:01:05,588 --> 00:01:10,916
That is called risk per trade, usually a small percent of the account.

12
00:01:11,916 --> 00:01:15,783
Then the lot size follows. The formula is simple.

13
00:01:16,232 --> 00:01:24,434
Size equals account times risk percent, divided by stop distance times the value of one point.

14
00:01:24,884 --> 00:01:31,886
The top part is the money at risk. The bottom part is the loss per lot.

15
00:01:32,886 --> 00:01:38,764
Say the account is 100,000. Risk 1%, which is 1,000.

16
00:01:39,214 --> 00:01:45,169
The stop is 50 points away, and one point is worth 10 per lot.

17
00:01:45,619 --> 00:01:50,374
So the loss per lot is 50 times 10, which is 500.

18
00:01:50,824 --> 00:01:55,107
1,000 divided by 500 is 2 lots. That is it.

19
00:01:56,107 --> 00:01:59,661
Now the next trade has a stop 100 points away.

20
00:02:00,110 --> 00:02:05,100
The loss per lot is 1,000, so the size is just 1 lot.

21
00:02:05,550 --> 00:02:11,819
Wider stop, smaller size. But the loss if wrong stays the same 1,000.

22
00:02:12,269 --> 00:02:16,710
That is real control. Every trade hurts the same.

23
00:02:17,710 --> 00:02:22,778
Some people always trade a fixed lot. Two lots, no matter what.

24
00:02:23,227 --> 00:02:29,994
The problem: the account shrinks, the risk does not. The same big bet even in drawdown.

25
00:02:30,444 --> 00:02:36,974
With fixed percent, a smaller account means a smaller bet automatically. A bigger one, a bigger bet.

26
00:02:37,974 --> 00:02:43,303
Back to the hook. After ten losses in a row, how much is left?

27
00:02:43,753 --> 00:02:47,724
At 1% risk: down about 10%.

28
00:02:48,174 --> 00:02:51,570
At 2%: about 18%.

29
00:02:52,019 --> 00:02:54,710
At 5%: 40%.

30
00:02:55,160 --> 00:03:02,475
And at 10%: 65%. As we saw last time, coming back from there is very hard.

31
00:03:03,475 --> 00:03:09,900
There is also a famous formula, Kelly. It says how much to bet for maximum growth.

32
00:03:10,351 --> 00:03:16,542
Kelly equals win rate, minus loss rate divided by payoff.

33
00:03:16,992 --> 00:03:23,757
Fifty percent win rate, payoff two: 50 minus 25, which is 25%.

34
00:03:24,208 --> 00:03:32,959
Twenty five percent per trade is very dangerous. If the estimates are even slightly off, the account is gone.

35
00:03:33,409 --> 00:03:40,096
So sensible traders use only a small fraction of Kelly, a quarter or even less.

36
00:03:41,096 --> 00:03:46,085
Another trap. Five open trades, 1% each.

37
00:03:46,535 --> 00:03:53,301
If they all point the same way, like all against the dollar, it is really one big bet.

38
00:03:53,751 --> 00:03:59,472
If the dollar turns, all five lose together. Five percent in one hit.

39
00:03:59,922 --> 00:04:03,553
So also cap your total open risk.

40
00:04:04,553 --> 00:04:06,120
So, the practical rules.

41
00:04:06,570 --> 00:04:10,280
Usually half to one percent per trade.

42
00:04:10,730 --> 00:04:15,876
Count related trades as one, and keep total open risk limited.

43
00:04:16,326 --> 00:04:21,002
And recalculate the size before every trade, never by feel.

44
00:04:22,002 --> 00:04:23,726
So, today's summary.

45
00:04:24,176 --> 00:04:31,020
Decide the risk first, then the lot. Size is money at risk divided by loss per lot.

46
00:04:31,470 --> 00:04:37,269
Fixed percent beats fixed lot. The bet shrinks by itself in drawdown.

47
00:04:37,719 --> 00:04:42,238
Never bet full Kelly, and treat related trades as one bet.

48
00:04:43,238 --> 00:04:49,769
Next episode: backtesting basics. How to test a system the right way.

49
00:04:50,219 --> 00:04:55,130
